 "_note": "Meatball Explains #3 (Showrunner, morning run 2026-10-25), for Sat 10/31 2 PM after Explains-01 moved to 10/25. One story, five hosts, Toxoplasma payoff promised in the first line and delivered last (open loop). Halloween-adjacent and true; no quiz, no checkpoints, no read-out (asks only). Voice kokoro:eric-michael (Nolan 10/24 free long-form voice). Shorts are not posted from long-forms (only v3/v4 Shorts post), so render with --skip-shorts. Contested claims are said as contested on air (Toxoplasma effects on people). Photos: iNaturalist research-grade, wild, CC0/CC BY, first photo of each obs, checked by eye. Fact pass 10/25 (Studio): wasp size 'smaller than a paper clip' (~22 mm), crickets 'terrible swimmers' (not 'can't swim'), hairworm feeds from inside (absorbs, doesn't chew organs), Toxoplasma 'a quarter to a third' (pooled ~26%), weak immune systems added, outro no longer says the wasp lacks a brain. | 10/25 Studio: re-cut as a v4 16:9 long-form with the early ask layout (OPERATIONS.md 'Always ask'): cold open, guess card ~0:20, one subscribe pill ~0:31, one like ask mid, 'Were you right?' at the end; in-story comment asks and the second subscribe ask removed; facts unchanged. | 10/25 Retention desk (research/47, tools/retention.py): agenda intro line replaced by a countdown promise ('Five zombie animals, counting down... the last one might be you'), 'I'm Meatball' moved into the subscribe line, on-screen 1 OF 5 counter + progress bar, payoff flagged, end-of-chapter teases; facts unchanged.",
 "sources": [
  "Ophiocordyceps: fungal cells surround and invade ant muscle fibres, brain not invaded — Fredericksen et al. 2017, PNAS, https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1711673114",
  "Death grip on leaf veins ~25 cm above ground, synchronised near solar noon — Hughes et al. 2011, BMC Ecology, https://bmcecol.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1472-6785-11-13",
  "48-million-year-old fossil leaf with death-grip scars (Messel) — Hughes, Wappler & Labandeira 2010, Biology Letters, https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rsbl.2010.0521",
  "Ampulex compressa: thorax sting then brain (subesophageal ganglion) sting, stinger senses target, roach walks but won't escape, led by antenna, egg on leg, burrow sealed, larva eats roach from inside — Libersat & Gal 2013/2014 (J Exp Biol; Frontiers), https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ampulex_compressa",
  "Hairworm (Spinochordodes tellinii) makes grasshoppers/crickets jump into water — Thomas et al. 2002, Proc R Soc B, https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rspb.2002.1971",
  "Leucochloridium pulsating broodsacs in eye tentacles; infected snails more exposed/higher and more active in light — Wesołowska & Wesołowski 2014, J Zoology, https://zslpublications.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/jzo.12094",
  "Toxoplasma: infected rats attracted to cat odour — Berdoy, Webster & Macdonald 2000, Proc R Soc B, https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rspb.2000.1182",
